{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-86bc3aba0e653dd5", "type": "tool_result", "content": "# ProDiff: Progressive Fast Diffusion Model For High-Quality Text-to-Speech\narXiv: 2207.06389\nAuthors: Rongjie Huang, Zhou Zhao, Huadai Liu, Jinglin Liu, Chenye Cui, Yi Ren\nYear: 2022 | Venue: \nTopic: tts_vc\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We analyze and compare different diffusion parameterizations in text-to-speech synthesis, showing that directly predicting clean data accelerates sampling better than estimating the gradient.\n  2. We propose ProDiff, which parameterizes the denoising model to directly predict clean data and uses knowledge distillation to reduce data variance for sharper predictions.\n  3. We demonstrate that ProDiff requires only 2 iterations to synthesize high-fidelity mel-spectrograms while maintaining competitive quality and diversity, enabling practical real-time deployment.\n\nTags: TTS, TTS/架构-NAR-Diffusion, TTS/功能-蒸馏加速, TTS/功能-快速生成", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "4ddf257c-e5d1-46e8-aa8c-f0cdb82a98e3", "uuid": "62cef42a-b7b6-4f1c-9252-5add21ef0d9f", "timestamp": "2026-07-29T10:28:15.182Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "# ProDiff: Progressive Fast Diffusion Model For High-Quality Text-to-Speech\narXiv: 2207.06389\nAuthors: Rongjie Huang, Zhou Zhao, Huadai Liu, Jinglin Liu, Chenye Cui, Yi Ren\nYear: 2022 | Venue: \nTopic: tts_vc\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We analyze and compare different diffusion parameterizations in text-to-speech synthesis, showing that directly predicting clean data accelerates sampling better than estimating the gradient.\n  2. We propose ProDiff, which parameterizes the denoising model to directly predict clean data and uses knowledge distillation to reduce data variance for sharper predictions.\n  3. We demonstrate that ProDiff requires only 2 iterations to synthesize high-fidelity mel-spectrograms while maintaining competitive quality and diversity, enabling practical real-time deployment.\n\nTags: TTS, TTS/架构-NAR-Diffusion, TTS/功能-蒸馏加速, TTS/功能-快速生成", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-9f2ed328f9fad3a3", "type": "tool_result", "content": "# On the Emotion Understanding of Synthesized Speech\narXiv: 2603.16483\nAuthors: Yuan Ge, Haishu Zhao, Aokai Hao, Junxiang Zhang, Bei Li, Xiaoqian Liu, Chenglong Wang, Jianjin Wang, Bingsen Zhou, Bingyu Liu, Jingbo Zhu, Zhengtao Yu, Tong Xiao\nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: speech_other\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Discriminative Speech Emotion Recognition (SER) models generalize poorly to synthesized speech, exhibiting a clear performance gap compared to human speech.\n  2. The dominant source of the synthesized domain shift is the speech token prediction stage, whereas subsequent flow matching and vocoder stages contribute substantially less.\n  3. Supervised and domain-adversarial fine-tuning fail to fundamentally resolve the generalization gap, indicating that existing SER models rely on non-robust shortcuts rather than capturing fundamental features.\n  4. Generative Speech Language Models tend to infer emotion primarily from textual semantics while ignoring paralinguistic cues, a behavior that persists even with prompt engineering.\n\nTags: Eval, Eval/听觉感知, LALM, TTS", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "4ddf257c-e5d1-46e8-aa8c-f0cdb82a98e3", "uuid": "5bb5984f-e427-40be-af4d-c37f82615ffa", "timestamp": "2026-07-29T10:28:15.521Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "# On the Emotion Understanding of Synthesized Speech\narXiv: 2603.16483\nAuthors: Yuan Ge, Haishu Zhao, Aokai Hao, Junxiang Zhang, Bei Li, Xiaoqian Liu, Chenglong Wang, Jianjin Wang, Bingsen Zhou, Bingyu Liu, Jingbo Zhu, Zhengtao Yu, Tong Xiao\nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: speech_other\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Discriminative Speech Emotion Recognition (SER) models generalize poorly to synthesized speech, exhibiting a clear performance gap compared to human speech.\n  2. The dominant source of the synthesized domain shift is the speech token prediction stage, whereas subsequent flow matching and vocoder stages contribute substantially less.\n  3. Supervised and domain-adversarial fine-tuning fail to fundamentally resolve the generalization gap, indicating that existing SER models rely on non-robust shortcuts rather than capturing fundamental features.\n  4. Generative Speech Language Models tend to infer emotion primarily from textual semantics while ignoring paralinguistic cues, a behavior that persists even with prompt engineering.\n\nTags: Eval, Eval/听觉感知, LALM, TTS", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b89a7e59b1f97dc0", "type": "tool_result", "content": "# Minimizing Modality Gap from the Input Side: Your Speech LLM Can Be a Prosody-Aware Text LLM\narXiv: 2605.05927\nAuthors: \nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: audio_understanding\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Proposes TextPro-SLM, a speech LLM that minimizes the text-speech modality gap from the input side by making spoken input resemble that of a prosody-aware text LLM.\n  2. Introduces WhisperPro, a unified speech encoder that produces synchronized text tokens and prosody embeddings to capture both linguistic content and paralinguistic information.\n  3. Trains the LLM backbone with coupled objectives to preserve the original text LLM's semantic capabilities via knowledge distillation while learning paralinguistic understanding.\n  4. Achieves the lowest modality gap among leading SLMs at both 3B and 7B scales while delivering strong performance on paralinguistic understanding tasks.\n  5. Demonstrates data efficiency by requiring only roughly 1,000 hours of audio for LLM training to effectively reduce the modality gap.\n\nTags: LALM, LALM/理解型", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "4ddf257c-e5d1-46e8-aa8c-f0cdb82a98e3", "uuid": "2559f884-284c-4598-83ab-07818d41433d", "timestamp": "2026-07-29T10:28:15.877Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "# Minimizing Modality Gap from the Input Side: Your Speech LLM Can Be a Prosody-Aware Text LLM\narXiv: 2605.05927\nAuthors: \nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: audio_understanding\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Proposes TextPro-SLM, a speech LLM that minimizes the text-speech modality gap from the input side by making spoken input resemble that of a prosody-aware text LLM.\n  2. Introduces WhisperPro, a unified speech encoder that produces synchronized text tokens and prosody embeddings to capture both linguistic content and paralinguistic information.\n  3. Trains the LLM backbone with coupled objectives to preserve the original text LLM's semantic capabilities via knowledge distillation while learning paralinguistic understanding.\n  4. Achieves the lowest modality gap among leading SLMs at both 3B and 7B scales while delivering strong performance on paralinguistic understanding tasks.\n  5. Demonstrates data efficiency by requiring only roughly 1,000 hours of audio for LLM training to effectively reduce the modality gap.\n\nTags: LALM, LALM/理解型", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
